+*   **Step:** Use four MMX instructions: `PMULHW`, `PMULLW`, `PMADDWD`, and `PSUBW` to create a tri-linear interpolation version taking only 37 instructions [S4].
+*   **Pitfall:** Performance on Pentium-II does not improve and may worsen compared to non-MMX version [S4].
+*   **Performance:** On Pentium-II, MMX provides 0% performance improvement over non-MMX integer version; on Pentium, it provides 60-80% faster performance [S4].
 
 ### Symmetry Optimization for SDF Evaluation
-Evaluate only a subset of primitives based on object symmetry to reduce computational cost [S5, unofficial]. For example, evaluate only 4 gear pieces instead of 18, and within each gear, evaluate only a single tooth/dent instead of 12 [S5, unofficial]. It is used in the 'Sphere Gears' Shadertoy example (2019) as an optimization exercise [S5, unofficial].
+Evaluate only a subset of primitives based on object symmetry to reduce computational cost. For example, evaluate only 4 gear pieces instead of 18, and within each gear, evaluate only a single tooth/dent instead of 12 [S5, unofficial]. Used in the 'Sphere Gears' Shadertoy example (2019) as an optimization exercise [S5, unofficial].
 
 ### Ray Differentials for Texture Filtering
-A technique using ray differentials to do proper texture filtering in a raymarching context [S5, unofficial]. It ensures that procedural textures sampled during ray marching are filtered correctly to avoid aliasing, similar to how rasterization handles texture filtering. It is used in the 'Arlo' Shadertoy example (2015) for procedural modeling [S5, unofficial].
+A technique using ray differentials to do proper texture filtering in a raymarching context [S5, unofficial]. Used in the 'Arlo' Shadertoy example (2015) for procedural modeling [S5, unofficial].
+
+### Ray Differentials for Antialiasing
+A technique to antialias screen-space edges of raymarched distance fields by estimating how much of the pixel footprint (pixel frustum) intersects the geometry using ray differentials [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Step:** Compute partial coverage per near-intersection and composite them front to back to give a correct smooth image without edge pixelization [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Used in the 'Antialias, sort of' Shadertoy example (2014) [S5, unofficial].
+
+### Wave Surfing Optimisations
+An optimization technique to reduce computation time in the rendering loop when the landscape is flat, by reducing time spent in interpolation between drawing operations [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Step:** Execute a technique in O(n) complexity that takes nearly constant steps through the height map [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Pitfall:** The rendering loop performs very badly if the landscape is completely flat, because every single column of voxels is higher than the previous and must be drawn [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Comparison:** A hierarchical visibility scheme (e.g., quadtree) is proposed as an ideal alternative to achieve O(logn) discard of invisible voxels, whereas the described O(n) technique takes nearly constant steps [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The optimizations reduce the average number of SDF evaluations per pixel. Early-outs and LOD reduce the constant factor in the loop. Resolution scaling reduces the total pixel count by 4x (for half resolution).
-- **Memory Complexity:** Pre-marched baking (S3, unofficial) increases memory usage by storing shadow and AO textures. MMX optimization (S4) has no additional memory cost.
-- **Measured Performance:**
-    - Replacing floating-point with integer arithmetic yields 4 to 6 times faster performance on older hardware (Pentium-II) [S4].
-    - Hand-coded address generation for tri-linear interpolation improves performance by up to 15% [S4].
-    - Rendering at half resolution is described as the "most effective single optimisation" for real-time SDF rendering at larger resolutions [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Significant frame rate improvements, especially on older hardware or for complex procedural SDFs.
-- **Limitations:** Over-relaxation introduces artifacts. Baking requires static scenes. MMX is obsolete on modern GPUs.
-- **When to use:** Real-time rendering of complex SDF scenes, VR applications, or hardware with limited ALU throughput.
-- **When not to use:** When visual precision is critical (avoid over-relaxation), or when the scene is dynamic and baking is not feasible.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Varies by optimization. Bounding volume early-outs reduce average case complexity by skipping sub-scenes. LOD reduces steps for distant objects. MMX optimization reduces instruction count for interpolation. Symmetry reduces primitive evaluations by a factor related to the symmetry order.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Baked textures (shadow/AO) increase memory usage proportional to the volume resolution. Half-resolution rendering reduces memory bandwidth usage for color/depth buffers.
+*   **Measured Performance:** On a 300-MHz Pentium-II, replacing floating-point with integer arithmetic yielded 4-6x speedup. MMX provided 0% improvement on Pentium-II but 60-80% on Pentium. Hand-coded address generation improved performance by up to 15% [S4].
+*   **Strengths:** Significant FPS gains in real-time applications. Effective for static scenes (baking) and symmetric objects.
+*   **Limitations:** Over-relaxation causes artifacts. Baking is limited to static scenes. MMX is obsolete on modern GPUs. Half-resolution can introduce aliasing if not upscaled properly.
 
 ## Background
-The techniques described originate from various sources in the ray marching community. Early-out bounding volumes and LOD strategies are discussed in [S1, unofficial] and [S2, unofficial]. The use of MMX for volume rendering was detailed in "I/O-Conscious Volume Rendering" [S4]. Symmetry and ray differentials were popularized by Inigo Quilez in his articles and Shadertoy examples [S5, unofficial]. Pre-marched baking for volumetric clouds was described in [S3, unofficial].
+The techniques described originate from various sources in real-time graphics, including Shadertoy examples (2014-2019) by artists like Inigo Quilez and others, blog posts on volumetric cloud rendering, and academic papers on I/O-conscious volume rendering for older hardware (Pentium era) [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4][S5, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. The core principles of bounding volume early-outs and LOD are widely adopted in modern SDF renderers to maintain real-time frame rates.
 
